#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""openclaw-eacces-lint — recurrence guard for the Linux ``.openclaw`` ACL-mask
EACCES bug class.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The bug class
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
On Linux/Ubuntu pods running Python 3.12, when the OpenClaw gateway (or an
``openclaw`` invocation run AS a bot user) hardens a bot's ``.openclaw`` dir to
0700, it re-clamps that dir's POSIX-ACL **mask** to ``---``, removing the
``evolve`` service user's inherited traverse ACE. A bare ``Path.exists()`` /
``Path.stat()`` / ``Path.is_file()`` / ``Path.is_dir()`` / ``open(...)`` on a
path UNDER that dir then **raises** ``PermissionError`` (Py3.12; ≤3.11 returned
``False``). Caught by a broad ``except Exception`` it silently aborts a deploy
(the live VPS "0 of N bots upgraded" blocker, #3184); uncaught it crashes a
daemon (``pod_perms_drift_monitor``, #2... W10-G round-6).

The codified fix lives in ``packages/admin/evolve_admin/secret_config_perms.py``:

  • existence checks → ``exists_or_unreachable(path)`` (catches ``OSError`` →
    returns ``True`` = "present / unreachable, let the privileged write proceed");
  • reads → ``read_text()`` with an EACCES fallback to ``sudo /bin/cat``;
  • any of the above wrapped in an explicit ``try / except (PermissionError,
    OSError)`` that classifies present/absent correctly.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Why a mechanical guard (the gap this closes)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TWO manual sweeps (#3141 created ``exists_or_unreachable`` and applied it 4× in
deploy.py; #3145's follow-up swept again) BOTH missed the single most-used
config-write site — ``safe_write_bot_config``'s ``config_path.exists()`` (the
funnel every bot ``openclaw.json`` write passes through). #3184 fixed that one
line by hand. Manual sweeps are demonstrably unreliable for this class, so this
lint freezes the current set of bare calls and BLOCKS any new one.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What is flagged
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
A *candidate* is one of these calls whose target resolves to a bot ``.openclaw``
path (heuristics below):

  X.exists()    X.stat()    X.is_file()    X.is_dir()
  open(X, ...)    os.open(X, ...)    io.open(X, ...)

A candidate is a *violation* unless it is **guarded** — lexically inside the
``try`` body of a ``try / except`` whose handler names ``PermissionError`` /
``OSError`` (or the ``IOError`` / ``EnvironmentError`` aliases). A bare
``except:`` and a broad ``except Exception`` do **NOT** count as guards — that
broad swallow is precisely the #3184 bug (it converts the raise into a silent
deploy-abort), not the fix. Routing through ``exists_or_unreachable(path)`` is
inherently safe because it is a plain function call, not a ``.exists()`` method
call, so it never matches a candidate; likewise a read rewritten to
``sudo /bin/cat`` removes the bare ``open()`` entirely.

"Resolves to a bot ``.openclaw`` path" (proximity heuristic) is true when the
target expression's AST subtree contains, anywhere:

  • an identifier (variable name / attribute) whose lower-case form contains a
    NAME needle — ``openclaw``, ``oc_json``, ``oc_dir``, ``oc_config``,
    ``oc_path``, ``oc_file``, ``oc_bak``, ``auth_profile`` / ``authprofile``; or
  • a string literal (incl. f-string fragments) containing a STRING needle —
    ``.openclaw``, ``auth-profiles.json``, ``agents/main``; or
  • a local variable previously assigned from such an expression — the
    assignment chain is resolved per-scope to a fixpoint, so

        oc_dir = _user_home(bot_user) / ".openclaw"      # string needle
        path   = oc_dir / rel                            # inherits via oc_dir
        if path.exists():  ...                           # ← flagged

    is caught even though ``path`` carries no needle of its own. This is the
    exact shape of the #3184 funnel
    (``config_path = _user_home(...) / ".openclaw/openclaw.json"``).

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Known heuristic limits (documented, à la the platform-blind lint #3173)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Detection is name/string-proximity + per-scope assignment tracking — NOT a type
checker or data-flow analysis. Consequences, accepted by design:

  • A ``.openclaw`` path threaded through a helper return value, a dict, or a
    function PARAMETER with a non-needle name is NOT recognised (false NEGATIVE).
    The blessed pattern (resolve the path locally, then check) is what the funnel
    sites use, so the high-value cases are covered.
  • A non-``.openclaw`` path that happens to live in a variable named e.g.
    ``oc_path`` would match (false POSITIVE). Such a hit is wrapped in
    ``try / except OSError`` or routed through ``exists_or_unreachable`` to clear
    it — both are harmless, correct hardening regardless of the path, so a rare
    false positive costs only a defensive wrap.
  • Treating a broad ``except Exception`` as NOT-a-guard is deliberate; it is the
    only way this lint catches the #3184 shape. A site that genuinely wants the
    broad handler still must funnel existence through ``exists_or_unreachable``.

Scope is ``packages/admin/`` + ``packages/analyzer/`` Python, minus test files
(tests pin both the broken and the fixed patterns as fixtures). Counts are
frozen per-file in ``tools/openclaw-eacces-baseline.txt`` and may only ratchet
DOWN. Goal state: empty.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Usage
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  tools/openclaw-eacces-lint <file.py> [...]        # lint specific files
  tools/openclaw-eacces-lint --all                  # lint all in scope (CI)
  tools/openclaw-eacces-lint --staged               # lint git-staged (pre-commit)
  tools/openclaw-eacces-lint --all --update-baseline  # re-freeze (shrink only)

Exit status: 0 if no file exceeds its baseline; 1 if a new bare call appeared.

To fix a hit, in order of preference:
  1. existence check   → secret_config_perms.exists_or_unreachable(path)
  2. read              → path.read_text() with an except → sudo /bin/cat fallback
  3. unavoidable       → try / except (PermissionError, OSError): <classify>
If a hit is genuinely unavoidable, bump tools/openclaw-eacces-baseline.txt in
this PR so the exception is reviewable.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
BASELINE_PATH = REPO_ROOT / "tools" / "openclaw-eacces-baseline.txt"

# Roots walked by --all. The bug is admin/analyzer (evolve-side) code touching a
# bot's .openclaw; in-bot app code runs AS the bot and is out of class.
_LINT_ROOTS = ("packages/admin", "packages/analyzer")

# ── openclaw-path heuristics ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Substrings (case-insensitive) in an identifier that mark a .openclaw path var.
_NAME_NEEDLES = (
    "openclaw", "oc_json", "oc_dir", "oc_config", "oc_path", "oc_file",
    "oc_bak", "auth_profile", "authprofile",
)
# Specific string literals (incl. f-string fragments) that mark a path inside a
# bot's .openclaw tree. ``.openclaw`` itself is matched separately (see
# _str_is_oc) so it only counts as a directory COMPONENT — the launchd label
# ``ai.openclaw.<bot>-gateway.plist`` embeds the substring ``.openclaw.`` but
# lives in /Library/LaunchDaemons, never under the clamped bot dir.
_STRING_NEEDLES = ("auth-profiles.json", "agents/main")
_OC_DIR = ".openclaw"

# Methods whose receiver is the candidate path.
_PATH_PROBE_METHODS = frozenset({"exists", "stat", "is_file", "is_dir"})
# Exception types that count as a real EACCES guard (PermissionError ⊂ OSError).
_GUARD_EXC_NAMES = frozenset({"OSError", "PermissionError", "IOError", "EnvironmentError"})


def _is_test_path(rel: str) -> bool:
    parts = rel.replace("\\", "/").split("/")
    if any(p in ("tests", "test") for p in parts):
        return True
    base = parts[-1]
    return base.startswith("test_") or base == "conftest.py"


def _name_is_oc(ident: str) -> bool:
    # Segment-anchored: a needle must begin at the start of the identifier or
    # right after ``_`` — so ``oc_path`` matches but ``doc_path`` / ``proc_dir``
    # (where ``oc_…`` is buried mid-segment) do not. snake_case is the norm here.
    low = ident.lower()
    for n in _NAME_NEEDLES:
        i = low.find(n)
        while i != -1:
            if i == 0 or low[i - 1] == "_":
                return True
            i = low.find(n, i + 1)
    return False


def _str_is_oc(s: str) -> bool:
    if any(n in s for n in _STRING_NEEDLES):
        return True
    # ``.openclaw`` only as a path component: followed by ``/`` or end-of-string.
    # Excludes the launchd label form ``ai.openclaw.<bot>-gateway`` (``.openclaw.``).
    idx = 0
    while True:
        i = s.find(_OC_DIR, idx)
        if i < 0:
            return False
        end = i + len(_OC_DIR)
        nxt = s[end] if end < len(s) else ""
        if nxt in ("", "/"):
            return True
        idx = end


# Path-producing constructs — used to keep taint propagation STRUCTURE-aware so
# a tainted value used as a filename segment doesn't poison an unrelated dir.
_PATH_PROP_ATTRS = frozenset({"parent"})  # attribute access yielding a path
_PATH_METHODS = frozenset({
    "with_suffix", "with_name", "with_stem", "joinpath",
    "resolve", "absolute", "expanduser",
})
# Home-dir resolvers — return a home, NOT a path under .openclaw on their own;
# only ``<home> / ".openclaw"`` is. Listed so they DON'T taint the bare home var.
_HOME_FUNCS = frozenset({"_user_home", "user_home", "bot_home", "_bot_home"})


def _str_node_is_oc(node: ast.AST) -> bool:
    """True if a string / f-string literal node carries a .openclaw needle."""
    if isinstance(node, ast.Constant) and isinstance(node.value, str):
        return _str_is_oc(node.value)
    if isinstance(node, ast.JoinedStr):
        return any(
            isinstance(v, ast.Constant) and isinstance(v.value, str) and _str_is_oc(v.value)
            for v in node.values
        )
    return False


def _is_path_ctor(call: ast.Call) -> bool:
    f = call.func
    return (isinstance(f, ast.Name) and f.id == "Path") or (
        isinstance(f, ast.Attribute) and f.attr == "Path"
    )


def _oc_path(node: ast.AST, oc_vars: set[str]) -> bool:
    """True if *node* evaluates to a path UNDER a bot ``.openclaw`` tree.

    Structure-aware (not a flat subtree scan): a ``a / b`` join is openclaw only
    via its *base* (``a``) or a ``.openclaw`` dir-STRING segment — never via a
    tainted variable used as a value/filename segment (the ``draft → pkg_id →
    gallery/…/{pkg_id}.json`` over-taint). Path-producing wrappers (``Path(…)``,
    ``.parent``, ``.with_suffix(…)``, …) recurse into their receiver/argument."""
    if isinstance(node, ast.Name):
        return node.id in oc_vars or _name_is_oc(node.id)
    if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute):
        if _name_is_oc(node.attr):
            return True
        if node.attr in _PATH_PROP_ATTRS:
            return _oc_path(node.value, oc_vars)
        return False
    if isinstance(node, ast.BinOp) and isinstance(node.op, ast.Div):
        return _oc_path(node.left, oc_vars) or _str_node_is_oc(node.right)
    if isinstance(node, ast.Call):
        f = node.func
        if _is_path_ctor(node):
            return any(_str_node_is_oc(a) or _oc_path(a, oc_vars) for a in node.args)
        if isinstance(f, ast.Attribute) and f.attr in _PATH_METHODS:
            return _oc_path(f.value, oc_vars)
        return False  # home resolvers + every other call: not under .openclaw alone
    if isinstance(node, ast.IfExp):  # ``a if cond else b`` — oc if either branch is
        return _oc_path(node.body, oc_vars) or _oc_path(node.orelse, oc_vars)
    return _str_node_is_oc(node)


def _assign_targets(node: ast.AST) -> list[str]:
    """Simple ``Name`` targets bound by an assignment statement."""
    out: list[str] = []
    if isinstance(node, ast.Assign):
        for t in node.targets:
            if isinstance(t, ast.Name):
                out.append(t.id)
            elif isinstance(t, (ast.Tuple, ast.List)):
                out.extend(e.id for e in t.elts if isinstance(e, ast.Name))
    elif isinstance(node, ast.AnnAssign) and isinstance(node.target, ast.Name):
        out.append(node.target.id)
    elif isinstance(node, ast.NamedExpr) and isinstance(node.target, ast.Name):
        out.append(node.target.id)
    return out


def _assign_value(node: ast.AST) -> ast.AST | None:
    if isinstance(node, (ast.Assign, ast.AnnAssign, ast.NamedExpr)):
        return node.value
    return None


# ── scope partitioning ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each FunctionDef/AsyncFunctionDef/ClassDef is its own scope so a generic name
# like ``path`` flagged in one function never leaks into a sibling. Returns
# (scope_root, nodes_owned_by_scope) where owned nodes exclude nested scopes.
def _scopes(tree: ast.AST) -> list[tuple[ast.AST, list[ast.AST]]]:
    scopes: list[tuple[ast.AST, list[ast.AST]]] = []

    def collect(node: ast.AST, own: list[ast.AST]) -> None:
        own.append(node)
        for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(node):
            if isinstance(child, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)):
                child_own: list[ast.AST] = []
                collect(child, child_own)
                scopes.append((child, child_own))
            else:
                collect(child, own)

    module_own: list[ast.AST] = []
    for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(tree):
        if isinstance(child, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)):
            child_own = []
            collect(child, child_own)
            scopes.append((child, child_own))
        else:
            collect(child, module_own)
    scopes.append((tree, module_own))
    return scopes


def _collect_oc_vars(own: list[ast.AST]) -> set[str]:
    """Fixpoint over a scope's assignments: a target is an oc-var if its RHS is
    an openclaw expression (which may itself reference earlier oc-vars)."""
    oc_vars: set[str] = set()
    for _ in range(12):  # bounded; chains in practice are 2-3 deep
        grew = False
        for node in own:
            value = _assign_value(node)
            if value is None:
                continue
            if _oc_path(value, oc_vars):
                for t in _assign_targets(node):
                    if t not in oc_vars:
                        oc_vars.add(t)
                        grew = True
        if not grew:
            break
    return oc_vars


# ── candidate + guard detection ──────────────────────────────────────────────
def _exc_names(node: ast.AST | None) -> list[str]:
    if node is None:
        return []
    if isinstance(node, ast.Name):
        return [node.id]
    if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute):
        return [node.attr]
    if isinstance(node, ast.Tuple):
        out: list[str] = []
        for e in node.elts:
            out.extend(_exc_names(e))
        return out
    return []


def _try_guards(try_node: ast.AST) -> bool:
    """True if any handler on *try_node* names an EACCES-catching exception.
    Bare ``except:`` / ``except Exception`` deliberately do NOT qualify."""
    for h in getattr(try_node, "handlers", []):
        for name in _exc_names(h.type):
            if name in _GUARD_EXC_NAMES:
                return True
    return False


def _candidate_target(call: ast.Call) -> ast.AST | None:
    """The path expression a probe/open call tests, or None if not a candidate."""
    fn = call.func
    if isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr in _PATH_PROBE_METHODS:
        return fn.value
    # open(X) / os.open(X) / io.open(X) — first positional arg, else file= kwarg.
    is_open = (isinstance(fn, ast.Name) and fn.id == "open") or (
        isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute)
        and fn.attr == "open"
        and isinstance(fn.value, ast.Name)
        and fn.value.id in ("os", "io")
    )
    if is_open:
        if call.args:
            return call.args[0]
        for kw in call.keywords:
            if kw.arg == "file":
                return kw.value
    return None


def _scope_violations(scope_root: ast.AST, oc_vars: set[str]) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    """(lineno, detail) for each unguarded openclaw probe/open in this scope."""
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    _TRY_TYPES = (ast.Try,) + ((ast.TryStar,) if hasattr(ast, "TryStar") else ())

    def visit(node: ast.AST, guarded: bool) -> None:
        # Stop at nested scopes — they are linted with their own oc_vars.
        if node is not scope_root and isinstance(
            node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)
        ):
            return
        if isinstance(node, ast.Call):
            target = _candidate_target(node)
            if target is not None and not guarded and _oc_path(target, oc_vars):
                fn = node.func
                if isinstance(fn, ast.Attribute) and fn.attr in _PATH_PROBE_METHODS:
                    detail = f".{fn.attr}() on a bot .openclaw path"
                else:
                    detail = "open() on a bot .openclaw path"
                out.append((node.lineno, detail))
        if isinstance(node, _TRY_TYPES):
            elevated = guarded or _try_guards(node)
            for stmt in node.body:            # only the try BODY is protected
                visit(stmt, elevated)
            for h in node.handlers:           # except/else/finally are not
                visit(h, guarded)
            for stmt in node.orelse:
                visit(stmt, guarded)
            for stmt in node.finalbody:
                visit(stmt, guarded)
            return
        for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(node):
            visit(child, guarded)

    visit(scope_root, False)
    return out


def _violations(path: Path) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
        return []
    out: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    for scope_root, own in _scopes(tree):
        oc_vars = _collect_oc_vars(own)
        out.extend(_scope_violations(scope_root, oc_vars))
    out.sort()
    return out


def _count(path: Path) -> int:
    return len(_violations(path))


# ── file selection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _iter_production_files() -> list[Path]:
    out: list[Path] = []
    for root in _LINT_ROOTS:
        for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(REPO_ROOT / root):
            if "__pycache__" in dirpath:
                continue
            for f in files:
                if not f.endswith(".py"):
                    continue
                p = Path(dirpath) / f
                rel = str(p.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
                if _is_test_path(rel):
                    continue
                out.append(p)
    return out


def _in_scope(rel: str) -> bool:
    rel = rel.replace("\\", "/")
    return rel.endswith(".py") and any(rel.startswith(r + "/") for r in _LINT_ROOTS)


def _staged_files() -> list[Path]:
    r = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=ACM"],
        cwd=REPO_ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    out = []
    for name in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if _in_scope(name) and not _is_test_path(name):
            p = REPO_ROOT / name
            if p.exists():
                out.append(p)
    return out


# ── baseline I/O ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _load_baseline(path: Path) -> dict[str, int]:
    if not path.exists():
        return {}
    baseline: dict[str, int] = {}
    for line in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        parts = line.split("\t", 1)  # "<count>\t<path>"
        if len(parts) != 2:
            continue
        try:
            baseline[parts[1]] = int(parts[0])
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return baseline


_HEADER = (
    "# openclaw-eacces-baseline — bare .exists()/.stat()/.is_file()/.is_dir()/",
    "# open() calls on a bot .openclaw path that are NOT guarded by",
    "# try/except (PermissionError, OSError) and do NOT route through",
    "# secret_config_perms.exists_or_unreachable(). On Linux/Py3.12 such a call",
    "# RAISES PermissionError when the OC gateway clamps the .openclaw ACL mask",
    "# to --- (the #3184 deploy-abort / W10-G daemon-crash class). Generated by",
    "# `tools/openclaw-eacces-lint --all --update-baseline`. The lint BLOCKS any",
    "# file that EXCEEDS its count, so this file may only ratchet DOWN: route the",
    "# call through exists_or_unreachable() / a read_text()+cat fallback / an",
    "# explicit except (PermissionError, OSError) -> rerun --update-baseline ->",
    "# the number drops. Goal state: this file is empty.",
)


def _write_baseline(counts: dict[str, int]) -> None:
    lines = list(_HEADER) + [""]
    for p in sorted(counts):
        if counts[p] > 0:
            lines.append(f"{counts[p]}\t{p}")
    BASELINE_PATH.write_text("\n".join(lines) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


def _rel(p: Path) -> str:
    try:
        return str(p.resolve().relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
    except ValueError:
        return str(p)


# ── runner ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def _targets(args: argparse.Namespace) -> list[Path]:
    if args.all:
        return _iter_production_files()
    if args.staged:
        return _staged_files()
    return [Path(f) for f in args.files if f.endswith(".py")]


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Recurrence guard for the Linux .openclaw ACL-mask EACCES bug class."
    )
    ap.add_argument("files", nargs="*", help="specific .py files to lint")
    ap.add_argument("--all", action="store_true", help="lint all in-scope .py")
    ap.add_argument("--staged", action="store_true", help="lint git-staged .py")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--update-baseline", action="store_true",
        help="regenerate the baseline from current counts (requires --all)",
    )
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.update_baseline:
        if not args.all:
            print("--update-baseline requires --all", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        counts = {_rel(p): _count(p) for p in _iter_production_files()}
        _write_baseline(counts)
        total = sum(counts.values())
        files = sum(1 for v in counts.values() if v)
        print(f"openclaw-eacces baseline written: {total} bare call(s) across {files} file(s)")
        return 0

    baseline = _load_baseline(BASELINE_PATH)
    violations: list[tuple[str, int, int, list[tuple[int, str]]]] = []
    below = 0
    for p in _targets(args):
        rel = _rel(p)
        if _is_test_path(rel) or not p.exists():
            continue
        hits = _violations(p)
        n = len(hits)
        allowed = baseline.get(rel, 0)
        if n > allowed:
            violations.append((rel, n, allowed, hits))
        elif n < allowed:
            below += 1

    if violations:
        print("✗ new bare existence/open call(s) on a bot .openclaw path:\n")
        for rel, n, allowed, hits in violations:
            print(f"  {rel}: {n} call(s), baseline allows {allowed}")
            for lineno, detail in hits:
                print(f"    L{lineno}: {detail}")
        print(
            "\nOn Linux/Py3.12 a bare .exists()/.stat()/.is_file()/.is_dir()/open()\n"
            "on a path under a bot .openclaw dir RAISES PermissionError when the OC\n"
            "gateway clamps the ACL mask to --- (the #3184 deploy-abort class).\n"
            "Fix, in order of preference:\n"
            "  1. existence check → secret_config_perms.exists_or_unreachable(path)\n"
            "  2. read           → path.read_text() with an except → sudo /bin/cat\n"
            "  3. unavoidable    → try / except (PermissionError, OSError): <classify>\n"
            "If a hit is genuinely unavoidable, bump tools/openclaw-eacces-baseline.txt\n"
            "in this PR so the exception is reviewable.\n"
        )
        return 1

    if below:
        print(
            f"openclaw-eacces-lint: {below} file(s) now below baseline — run\n"
            "`tools/openclaw-eacces-lint --all --update-baseline` to ratchet down."
        )
    print("openclaw-eacces-lint: OK")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
